1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.240
[S01] Young people globally wanna be like Elon Musk.

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:04.719
[S01] What's your advice to them?

3
00:00:06.559 --> 00:00:10.640
[S02] I think that probably they shouldn't wanna be.

4
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:11.679
[S01] You?

5
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.839
[S02] I think it sounds better than it is.

6
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.239
[S02] Yeah, it's not as much fun being me as you'd think. I don't know.

7
00:00:22.239 --> 00:00:22.960
[S01] You don't think so?

8
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.839
[S02] There's definitely, it could be worse for sure, but it's...

9
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.520
[S02] I'm not sure I would, I'm not sure I wanna be me.

10
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.640
[S02] Depending upon how well you wanna do, particularly if you're starting a company,

11
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.840
[S02] you need to work super hard.

12
00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:40.240
[S02] So what does super hard mean?

13
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.240
[S02] Well, when my brother and I were starting our first company,

14
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.759
[S02] instead of getting an apartment, we just rented a small office and we slept on the couch.

15
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.200
[S02] And we showered at the YMCA.

16
00:00:53.759 --> 00:01:00.000
[S02] And we're so hot up, we had one computer, so the website was up during the day

17
00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:01.840
[S02] and I was coding at night.

18
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.230
[S02] Seven days a week, all the time.

19
00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.120
[S02] And I sort of briefly had a girlfriend in that period and in order to be with me,

20
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.230
[S02] she has to sleep in the office.

21
00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:19.120
[S02] So work hard like, I mean, every waking hour.

22
00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:23.439
[S02] That's the thing I would say, particularly if you're starting a company.

23
00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:29.120
[S02] And I mean, if you do simple math, say like, okay, if somebody else is working 50 hours

24
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.280
[S02] and you're working 100, you'll get twice as done, as much done in the course of a year

25
00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.959
[S02] as the other company.

26
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.719
[S02] So many people tried to talk me out of starting a right company. It was crazy.

27
00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:43.840
[S02] One good friend of mine collected a whole series of videos of rockets blowing up

28
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.480
[S02] and made me watch those. He just didn't want me to lose all my money.

29
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.480
[S02] We're doing these things that seem unlikely to succeed.

30
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:54.079
[S02] And we've been fortunate and at least thus far, they have succeeded.

31
00:01:54.319 --> 00:01:59.840
[S03] This is widely regarded as one of the most robotics-driven auto assembly lines on the planet.

32
00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:04.480
[S04] Elon, part of the thing I heard about the Model 3 is that there's too many robots that maybe...

33
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:05.120
[S02] Yeah, yeah, I agree.

34
00:02:05.519 --> 00:02:08.400
[S04] You think so too? That maybe you need more people in here working?

35
00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:08.800
[S02] We do.

36
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.159
[S04] In some cases, the robots actually slowed the production, right?

37
00:02:12.159 --> 00:02:12.629
[S02] Yes, they did.

38
00:02:12.879 --> 00:02:17.120
[S02] We had this crazy, complex network of conveyor belts and it was not working.

39
00:02:17.439 --> 00:02:19.039
[S02] So we got rid of that whole thing.

40
00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:20.469
[S04] This is cool, Elon.

41
00:02:20.469 --> 00:02:20.879
[S02] Yeah.

42
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.439
[S03] Realizing it needed an overhaul, Musk personally took over the Model 3

43
00:02:25.439 --> 00:02:27.599
[S03] production line at the beginning of April.

44
00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.469
[S02] We're aiming for really extreme levels of precision, more than any other vehicle in the world.

45
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.840
[S03] He says he has resorted to pulling all-nighters at the plant.

46
00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.039
[S02] When things get really intense, I don't have time to go home and shower and change,

47
00:02:39.039 --> 00:02:39.759
[S02] so I just sleep here.

48
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.039
[S04] I wanna see, where is that?

49
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:42.879
[S02] Oh yeah, it's in this comfort room.

50
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:45.840
[S02] I mean, it's pretty boring overall, really.

51
00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.960
[S04] It's actually cold in here, too.

52
00:02:51.039 --> 00:02:52.000
[S02] Yeah, I like it cold.

53
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.520
[S04] So you have a, you like it cold?

54
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:54.560
[S02] I sleep on the couch over there.

55
00:02:54.639 --> 00:02:56.560
[S04] So you're just laying here on the couch?

56
00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.039
[S02] Yeah. Last time I was here, I actually slept literally on the floor

57
00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:00.159
[S02] because the couch was too narrow.

58
00:03:00.639 --> 00:03:01.439
[S04] Yeah, I was gonna say.

59
00:03:02.479 --> 00:03:05.199
[S04] And Elon, I have to say, it's not even a comfortable couch either.

60
00:03:05.199 --> 00:03:07.120
[S02] No, it's terrible. This is not a good couch.

61
00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:10.080
[S03] Musk feels like all the overtime is paying off.

62
00:03:10.479 --> 00:03:13.840
[S03] And now he says the Model 3 line is back on track.

63
00:03:13.919 --> 00:03:16.800
[S02] And we're able to unlock some of the critical things that were holding us back

64
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.840
[S02] from reaching 2,000 cars a week.

65
00:03:20.159 --> 00:03:22.479
[S02] But since then, we've continued to do 2,000 cars a week.

66
00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.560
[S04] Do you think that this is sustainable?

67
00:03:25.199 --> 00:03:26.479
[S04] This pace is sustainable?

68
00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:31.120
[S02] One does have to be focused on, for short-term money coming in when creating a company,

69
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.960
[S02] because otherwise the company will die.

70
00:03:33.919 --> 00:03:41.439
[S02] So the, I think that a lot of times people think like creating a company is gonna be fun.

71
00:03:41.439 --> 00:03:43.199
[S02] I would say it's not, it's really not that fun.

72
00:03:43.199 --> 00:03:47.360
[S02] I mean, there are periods of fun and there are periods of where it's just awful.

73
00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.800
[S02] And particularly if you're the CEO of the company,

74
00:03:52.479 --> 00:03:55.439
[S02] you actually have a distillation of all the worst problems in the company.

75
00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.919
[S02] There's no point in spending your time on things that are going right.

76
00:04:00.479 --> 00:04:03.120
[S02] So you only spend on things, your time on things that are going wrong.

77
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.759
[S02] And there are things that are going wrong that other people can't take care of.

78
00:04:07.759 --> 00:04:08.960
[S02] So you're like the worst.

79
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:11.840
[S02] You have a filter for the crappiest problems in the company.

80
00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:14.719
[S02] Most pernicious and painful problem.

81
00:04:17.269 --> 00:04:20.879
[S02] So I wouldn't say it's, I think you have to feel quite compelled to do it

82
00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:25.040
[S02] and have a fairly high pain threshold.

83
00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:29.439
[S02] And there's a friend of mine who says like, starting a company is like staring into the abyss

84
00:04:29.439 --> 00:04:30.560
[S02] and eating glass.

85
00:04:32.879 --> 00:04:33.829
[S02] And there's some truth to that.

86
00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:42.160
[S02] The staring into the abyss part is that you're gonna be constantly facing the extermination

87
00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:42.639
[S02] of the company.

88
00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.639
[S02] Because most startups fail.

89
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:49.360
[S02] It's like 90%, arguably 99% of startups fail.

90
00:04:50.079 --> 00:04:53.829
[S02] So that's the staring into the abyss part.

91
00:04:53.829 --> 00:04:58.879
[S02] You're constantly saying, okay, if I don't get this right, the company will die.

92
00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:02.240
[S02] It's gonna be quite stressful, quite stressful.

93
00:05:02.879 --> 00:05:09.759
[S02] And then the eating glass part is, you've got to do, you've got to do the problems,

94
00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:12.000
[S02] you're gonna work on the problems that the company needs you to work on,

95
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:13.199
[S02] not the problems you wanna work on.

96
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:21.120
[S02] And so that's, you end up working on problems that you'd really wish you weren't working on.

97
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:04.720
[S05] I want to diminish the, I mean, I think the profit motive is a good one if the rules

98
00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.329
[S05] of an industry are properly set up.

99
00:06:06.329 --> 00:06:08.439
[S05] So there's nothing fundamentally wrong with profit.

100
00:06:08.439 --> 00:06:14.040
[S05] In fact, profit just means that people are paying you more for whatever you're doing,

101
00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.459
[S05] then you're spending to create it.

102
00:06:15.459 --> 00:06:18.819
[S05] That's a good thing.

103
00:06:18.819 --> 00:06:22.839
[S05] And if you're not, if that's not the case, then you'll be out of business and rightfully

104
00:06:22.839 --> 00:06:23.839
[S05] so.

105
00:06:23.839 --> 00:06:26.730
[S05] Because you're not adding enough value.

106
00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:31.610
[S06] So you're running, you're CEO of two companies, you're chairman of SolarCity.

107
00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:32.970
[S06] Talk about time management.

108
00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.610
[S06] How on earth do you do this?

109
00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.470
[S06] Do you get any sleep?

110
00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:40.819
[S05] Sometimes not enough.

111
00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:42.889
[S05] Sleep is really great.

112
00:06:42.889 --> 00:06:47.279
[S05] Because if, I find if I don't get enough sleep, then I'm quite grumpy.

113
00:06:47.279 --> 00:06:51.319
[S05] I mean, obviously, I think most people are that way.

114
00:06:51.319 --> 00:06:56.639
[S05] And also, I can try to sort of figure out what's the right amount of sleep.

115
00:06:56.639 --> 00:07:01.209
[S05] Because I found I could have, I could drop below a certain threshold of sleep, and although

116
00:07:01.209 --> 00:07:08.610
[S05] I'd be awake more hours and I could sustain it, I would get less done because my mental

117
00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.310
[S05] acuity would be affected.

118
00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:15.430
[S05] So I've found generally, the right number for me is around six to six and a half hours

119
00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:16.430
[S05] on average per night.

120
00:07:16.430 --> 00:07:18.589
[S06] That's not too bad.

121
00:07:18.589 --> 00:07:19.589
[S05] Yeah.

122
00:07:19.589 --> 00:07:20.589
[S06] And any other tips on...

123
00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:21.589
[S05] That is an average.

124
00:07:21.589 --> 00:07:22.589
[S06] Right.

125
00:07:22.589 --> 00:07:26.389
[S06] Any other tips on just managing to run two companies simultaneously?

126
00:07:26.389 --> 00:07:31.490
[S06] I mean, do you find, I mean, I know you're up here Monday, Tuesday, is it all Tesla

127
00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:36.110
[S06] when you're up in Silicon Valley and all SpaceX Wednesday, Thursday?

128
00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:43.220
[S05] Having a, sort of, having a smartphone is incredibly helpful because that means

129
00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:47.449
[S05] you can do email during interstitial periods, like if you're in a car, you're walking, in

130
00:07:47.449 --> 00:07:53.110
[S05] the bathroom, everywhere, you can do email practically when you're awake.

131
00:07:53.110 --> 00:08:02.529
[S05] And so that's really helpful, to have email for SpaceX and Tesla integrated on my phone.

132
00:08:02.529 --> 00:08:09.220
[S05] And then, and then it's just you have to apply a lot of hours to actual working, actually

133
00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:10.220
[S05] working.

134
00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:18.620
[S05] So the way I generally do it is I'll be working at SpaceX on Monday, and then Monday night,

135
00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:27.560
[S05] fly to the Bay Area, spend Tuesday and Wednesday at the Bay Area, then at Tesla, and then fly

136
00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:32.809
[S05] back on Wednesday night, spend Thursday and Friday at SpaceX.

137
00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:38.190
[S05] In the last several months, then I would fly back here on a Saturday, and either spend

138
00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:44.840
[S05] Saturday and Sunday at Tesla, or spend Saturday at Tesla and Sunday at SpaceX.

139
00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:48.120
[S07] Focus on signal over noise.

140
00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:51.149
[S07] A lot of companies get confused.

141
00:08:51.149 --> 00:08:55.309
[S07] They spend money on things that don't actually make the product better.

142
00:08:55.309 --> 00:09:01.120
[S07] For example, at Tesla, we've never spent any money on appetizing.

143
00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:06.679
[S07] We've put all of the money into R and D and manufacturing and design to try to make

144
00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:10.120
[S07] the car as good as possible.

145
00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:13.509
[S07] And I think that's the way to go.

146
00:09:13.509 --> 00:09:19.809
[S07] For any given company, just keep thinking about, are these efforts that people are

147
00:09:19.809 --> 00:09:22.840
[S07] expending, are they resulting in a better product or service?

148
00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:24.870
[S07] And if they're not, stop those efforts.

149
00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:30.620
[S08] Really liking what you do, whatever area that you get into, given that, you know,

150
00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:34.370
[S08] even if you're the best of the best, there's always a chance of failure.

151
00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:37.809
[S08] So I think it's important that you really like whatever you're doing.

152
00:09:37.809 --> 00:09:40.620
[S08] If you don't like it, life is too short.

153
00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.370
[S08] If you like what you're doing, you think about it even when you're not working.

154
00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:49.309
[S08] I mean, it'll just, it's something that your mind is drawn to.

155
00:09:49.309 --> 00:09:52.809
[S08] And if you don't like it, you just really can't make it work, I think.

156
00:09:52.809 --> 00:09:57.259
[S05] First of all, you don't need college to learn stuff, okay?

157
00:09:57.259 --> 00:09:59.980
[S05] Everything is available, basically, for free.

158
00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:02.730
[S05] You can learn anything you want for free.

159
00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:06.149
[S05] It is not a question of learning.

160
00:10:06.149 --> 00:10:13.620
[S05] There is a value that colleges have, which is like, you know, seeing whether somebody's,

161
00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:19.220
[S05] can somebody work hard at something, including a bunch of sort of annoying homework assignments,

162
00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:25.620
[S05] and still do their homework assignments, and kind of soldier through and get it done?

163
00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:30.019
[S05] You know, that's, that's like the main value of college.

164
00:10:30.019 --> 00:10:34.580
[S05] And then also, you know, if you probably want to hang around with a bunch of people

165
00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:39.309
[S05] your own age for a while, instead of going right into the workforce.

166
00:10:39.309 --> 00:10:43.519
[S05] So I think colleges are basically for fun and to prove you can do your chores, but

167
00:10:43.519 --> 00:10:44.519
[S05] they're not for learning.

168
00:10:44.519 --> 00:10:46.769
[S09] What was your biggest failure, and how did it change you?

169
00:10:46.769 --> 00:10:48.330
[S05] I had to really think hard about that.

170
00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:49.330
[S05] Failure.

171
00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:50.330
[S05] Never heard of it.

172
00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:51.330
[S09] There's your answer.

173
00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:52.830
[S05] Well, there's a ton of failures along the way, that's for sure.

174
00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:57.080
[S05] For SpaceX, first three launches failed, and we're just barely able to scrape together

175
00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:59.940
[S05] enough parts and money to do the fourth launch.

176
00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:01.620
[S05] If the fourth launch had failed, we would have been dead.

177
00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:03.309
[S05] So multiple failures along the way.

178
00:11:03.309 --> 00:11:06.519
[S05] I tried very hard to get the right expertise in for SpaceX.

179
00:11:06.519 --> 00:11:09.830
[S05] I tried hard to find a great chief engineer for the rocket, but the good chief engineers

180
00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:12.190
[S05] wouldn't join, and the bad ones, well, there was no point in hiring them.

181
00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:13.769
[S05] So I ended up being chief engineer of the rocket.

182
00:11:13.769 --> 00:11:17.110
[S05] So if I could have found somebody better, then we would have maybe had less than three

183
00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:18.110
[S05] failures.

184
00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:22.019
[S10] There were just times when something is important enough, you've levered it enough,

185
00:11:22.019 --> 00:11:24.519
[S10] that you do it in spite of fear.

186
00:11:24.519 --> 00:11:30.080
[S05] You have to imagine an outcome in order to head in that direction.

187
00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:36.379
[S05] And science fiction explores a lot of different ideas, so it can be helpful as a source of

188
00:11:36.379 --> 00:11:37.379
[S05] inspiration.

189
00:11:37.379 --> 00:11:45.679
[S05] And, you know, like, books, TV shows, movies, I mean, they're all, I think, sources of

190
00:11:45.679 --> 00:11:48.929
[S05] inspiration.

191
00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.539
[S11] An inspiration for the cellphone.

192
00:12:02.539 --> 00:12:03.940
[S12] Yeah, with the flip, right?

193
00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:05.120
[S11] Right, yeah, exactly.

194
00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:09.820
[S11] In fact, the weird thing is like the phones we have in our pocket

195
00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.419
[S11] vastly exceed what was on Star Trek.

196
00:12:12.419 --> 00:12:16.549
[S13] The real way I think you actually achieve intellectual property protection

197
00:12:16.549 --> 00:12:19.279
[S13] is by innovating fast enough.

198
00:12:19.279 --> 00:12:25.610
[S13] If your rate of innovation is high, then you don't need to worry about protecting the IP

199
00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:29.820
[S13] because other companies will be copying something that you did years ago.

200
00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:30.820
[S11] Mm-hmm.

201
00:12:30.820 --> 00:12:34.960
[S13] And that's fine, you know.

202
00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:40.440
[S13] Just make sure your rate of innovation is fast.

203
00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:44.980
[S13] Speed is really, speed of innovation is what matters.

204
00:12:44.980 --> 00:12:51.120
[S13] And I do say this to my teams like quite a lot, that innovation per unit time, as I go,

205
00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:57.299
[S13] innovation per year, if you want to say it, is what matters, not innovation absent time.

206
00:12:57.299 --> 00:13:02.919
[S13] Because if you wanted to make, say, a hundred percent improvement in something and that

207
00:13:02.919 --> 00:13:07.889
[S13] took a hundred years or one year, that's radically different.

208
00:13:07.889 --> 00:13:13.220
[S13] So it's like, what is your rate of innovation that matters?

209
00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:18.419
[S13] And is the rate of innovation, is that accelerating or decelerating?

210
00:13:18.419 --> 00:13:25.970
[S13] And a weird thing happens when companies get big is that most companies or organizations,

211
00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:32.139
[S13] the bigger they get, they tend to get less innovative.

212
00:13:32.139 --> 00:13:38.610
[S13] Not just less innovative on a per person basis, but less innovative in the absolute.

213
00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:48.610
[S13] And I think this is probably because the incentive structure is not, is not there for innovation.

214
00:13:48.610 --> 00:13:52.309
[S13] It's not enough to use words to encourage innovation.

215
00:13:52.309 --> 00:13:55.110
[S13] The incentive structure must be aligned with that.

216
00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:56.470
[S13] That's fundamental.

217
00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:57.889
[S13] I think failure is bad.

218
00:13:57.889 --> 00:14:00.019
[S13] I don't think it's good.

219
00:14:00.019 --> 00:14:05.220
[S13] But if something's important enough, then you do it even though the risk of failure

220
00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:07.720
[S13] is high.

221
00:14:07.720 --> 00:14:13.009
[S13] And so I think my advice if somebody wants to start a company is they should bear in

222
00:14:13.009 --> 00:14:16.940
[S13] mind that the most likely outcome is that it's not going to work.

223
00:14:16.940 --> 00:14:21.220
[S13] And they should reconcile themselves to that strong possibility.

224
00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:29.620
[S13] And they should only do it if they feel that they are really compelled to do it, you know?

225
00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:34.220
[S13] Because it's, it's gonna, the way starting a company works is like, usually in the beginning,

226
00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:37.289
[S13] it's, the very beginning, it's kind of fun.

227
00:14:37.289 --> 00:14:41.220
[S13] And then it's really hellish for a number of years.

228
00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.120
[S14] You're talking about chewing glass?

229
00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:52.009
[S13] Yeah, there's a friend of mine who's a successful entrepreneur and started actually

230
00:14:52.009 --> 00:14:53.700
[S13] his career around the same time as I did.

231
00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:57.649
[S13] And he has a good phrase, his name's Billy.

232
00:14:57.649 --> 00:15:02.809
[S13] He said, yeah, starting a company is like eating glass and staring into the abyss.

233
00:15:02.809 --> 00:15:04.620
[S14] And you agree with that?

234
00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.120
[S13] Generally true.

235
00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:08.649
[S13] Yeah.

236
00:15:08.649 --> 00:15:11.730
[S13] And if you don't eat the glass, you're not going to be successful.

237
00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:19.230
[S15] Why do we need to build a city on Mars with a million people on it in your lifetime?

238
00:15:19.230 --> 00:15:22.309
[S15] Which I think is kind of what you said you'd love to do.

239
00:15:22.309 --> 00:15:30.509
[S13] Yeah, I think it's important to have a future that is inspiring and appealing.

240
00:15:30.509 --> 00:15:34.009
[S13] I mean, I just think that there, like, there have to be reasons that you get up in the

241
00:15:34.009 --> 00:15:36.370
[S13] morning and you want to live.

242
00:15:36.370 --> 00:15:37.429
[S13] Like, why do you want to live?

243
00:15:37.429 --> 00:15:38.429
[S13] What's the point?

244
00:15:38.429 --> 00:15:39.429
[S13] What inspires you?

245
00:15:39.429 --> 00:15:41.539
[S13] What do you love about the future?

246
00:15:41.539 --> 00:15:45.259
[S13] And if we're not out there, if the future does not include being out there among the

247
00:15:45.259 --> 00:15:51.620
[S13] stars and being a multi-planet species, I find that it's incredibly depressing if that's

248
00:15:51.620 --> 00:15:53.899
[S13] not the future that we're going to have.

249
00:15:53.899 --> 00:16:00.649
[S13] I think the value of beauty and inspiration is very much underrated, no question.

250
00:16:00.649 --> 00:16:03.929
[S13] But I want to be clear, I'm not trying to be anyone's savior.

251
00:16:03.929 --> 00:16:10.429
[S13] That is not the, I'm just trying to think about the future and not be sad.

252
00:16:10.429 --> 00:16:17.750
[S13] Generally, you want to embark on something, it's desirable to figure out if success

253
00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:19.250
[S13] is at least one of the possibilities.

254
00:16:19.250 --> 00:16:20.250
[S16] Right, exactly.

255
00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:23.440
[S13] Because for sure, failure is one of the possibilities.

256
00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:24.440
[S16] Yes.

257
00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:30.769
[S13] But ideally, you want to try to bracket it and say, success is in the envelope

258
00:16:30.769 --> 00:16:31.769
[S13] of outcomes.

259
00:16:31.769 --> 00:16:32.769
[S16] Yeah.

260
00:16:32.769 --> 00:16:35.879
[S13] And I wasn't quite sure if that was the case.

261
00:16:35.879 --> 00:16:40.120
[S13] I mean, I think success on an academic level would have been quite likely because you

262
00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:41.830
[S13] can publish some useless paper.

263
00:16:41.830 --> 00:16:45.830
[S13] And most papers are pretty useless, you know?

264
00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:47.330
[S16] We have a few, don't take offense.

265
00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:52.269
[S13] I mean, how many PhD papers are actually used by someone ever?

266
00:16:52.269 --> 00:16:53.269
[S13] I mean.

267
00:16:53.269 --> 00:16:54.330
[S16] That's a good point.

268
00:16:54.330 --> 00:16:56.120
[S13] Percentage-wise, it's not, it's not good.

269
00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:57.120
[S16] Yeah.

270
00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:03.830
[S13] And so, so it could have been one of those outcomes where you add some leaves

271
00:17:03.830 --> 00:17:05.619
[S13] to the tree of knowledge.

272
00:17:05.619 --> 00:17:06.619
[S16] Yeah, yeah.

273
00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:07.619
[S13] Okay?

274
00:17:07.619 --> 00:17:09.019
[S13] And that leaf is, nope, it's not possible.

275
00:17:09.019 --> 00:17:10.019
[S16] Right.

276
00:17:10.019 --> 00:17:15.309
[S13] Like, there goes seven years of mine.

277
00:17:15.309 --> 00:17:17.579
[S13] So that was one path.

278
00:17:17.579 --> 00:17:20.769
[S13] And I was prepared to do that, but then the internet was, the internet came along and

279
00:17:20.769 --> 00:17:24.519
[S13] I was like, okay, the internet, I'm pretty sure success is one of the possible outcomes.

280
00:17:24.519 --> 00:17:30.000
[S13] And it seemed like I could either do a PhD and watch the internet happen, or I could

281
00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.500
[S13] participate and help build it in some fashion, you know?

282
00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:36.809
[S13] Like, I just couldn't stand the idea of watching it happen.

283
00:17:36.809 --> 00:17:37.809
[S16] Yeah, yeah.

284
00:17:37.809 --> 00:17:44.019
[S13] So that's, so I decided to put things on hold and start an internet company.

285
00:17:44.019 --> 00:17:51.019
[S13] And that was kind of a, we worked on internet, like publishing software, maps and directions,

286
00:17:51.019 --> 00:17:52.019
[S13] yellow pages, kind of things.

287
00:17:52.019 --> 00:17:56.809
[S17] I think it's also important to reason from first principles rather than by analogy.

288
00:17:56.809 --> 00:17:59.990
[S17] So the normal way that we conduct our lives is we reason by analogy.

289
00:18:00.079 --> 00:18:04.839
[S18] We're doing this because it's like something else that was done.

290
00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:08.440
[S18] Or it's like what other people are doing.

291
00:18:08.759 --> 00:18:10.029
[S19] Me too type ideas.

292
00:18:10.480 --> 00:18:14.240
[S18] Yeah, slight iterations on a theme.

293
00:18:15.880 --> 00:18:25.119
[S18] And it's kind of mentally easier to reason by analogy rather than from first principles.

294
00:18:25.119 --> 00:18:28.720
[S18] But first principles is kind of a physics way of looking at the world.

295
00:18:29.279 --> 00:18:34.640
[S18] And what that really means is you kind of boil things down to the most fundamental truths.

296
00:18:35.039 --> 00:18:39.200
[S18] And say, okay, what are we sure is true or as sure as possible is true?

297
00:18:40.359 --> 00:18:42.240
[S18] And then reason up from there.

298
00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:44.480
[S18] That takes a lot more mental energy.

299
00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:49.359
[S19] Give me an example of that, like what's one thing that you've done that on that you feel has worked for you?

300
00:18:49.759 --> 00:18:56.839
[S18] Sure, so somebody could say, and in fact, people do, that battery packs are really expensive.

301
00:18:56.839 --> 00:18:59.920
[S18] And that's just the way they will always be because that's the way they've been in the past.

302
00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:04.279
[S18] You're like, well, no, that's that's pretty dumb, you know?

303
00:19:04.279 --> 00:19:14.640
[S18] Because if you applied that reasoning to anything new, then you wouldn't be able to ever get to that new thing.

304
00:19:15.279 --> 00:19:19.680
[S18] One of the biggest mistakes people generally make, and I'm guilty of it too, is wishful thinking.

305
00:19:20.799 --> 00:19:24.319
[S18] You know, like you want something to be true, even if it isn't true.

306
00:19:25.759 --> 00:19:31.839
[S18] And so you ignore the things that you ignore the real truth because of what you want to be true.

307
00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:36.319
[S18] This is a very difficult trap to avoid.

308
00:19:37.039 --> 00:19:42.000
[S18] And like I said, it's certainly one that I find myself in having problems with.

309
00:19:43.279 --> 00:19:49.359
[S18] But if you just take that approach of you're always to some degree wrong and your goal is to be less wrong.

310
00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:53.920
[S18] And solicit critical feedback, particularly from friends.

311
00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:59.039
[S18] Like friends, particularly friends, if somebody loves you, they want the best for you.

312
00:19:59.599 --> 00:20:01.039
[S18] They don't want to tell you the bad things.

313
00:20:03.039 --> 00:20:07.599
[S18] So you have to ask them, you know, and say, really, I really do want to know.

314
00:20:12.319 --> 00:20:16.880
[S20] Freedom, a word so common that most of us rarely stop to think about it.

315
00:20:17.839 --> 00:20:21.440
[S20] Not because it disappeared, but because it was always there.

316
00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:26.240
[S20] Like clean air, like electricity, like the internet.

317
00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:30.400
[S20] The things we depend on most are usually the things we notice the least.

318
00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:34.960
[S20] And that's exactly why freedom is dangerous to lose, not when it's taken overnight.

319
00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:39.839
[S20] But when it disappears so slowly that nobody notices.

320
00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:45.440
[S20] More than 100 years ago, the passengers of the Titanic made a similar mistake.

321
00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:51.119
[S20] The ship struck an iceberg, yet many people stayed exactly where they were.

322
00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:55.839
[S20] They believed the Titanic was unsinkable. They believe there was still time.

323
00:20:55.920 --> 00:21:04.000
[S21] Passengers of the Titanic actually didn't want to leave the ship for almost two hours after it hit the iceberg.

324
00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:07.839
[S21] People thought the Titanic was unsinkable.

325
00:21:08.799 --> 00:21:11.680
[S21] Lifeboats left half empty.

326
00:21:13.839 --> 00:21:19.519
[S21] Only in the last half an hour, people started to panic, but by that time it was already too late.

327
00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:24.400
[S20] History teaches the same lesson over and over again.

328
00:21:24.880 --> 00:21:28.079
[S20] The greatest threats rarely arrive with alarms.

329
00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:36.559
[S20] They arrive with comfort, with convenience, with good intentions, and sometimes with promises of safety.

330
00:21:37.039 --> 00:21:38.880
[S20] Every generation is told the same thing.

331
00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:44.240
[S20] Give up a little freedom and you'll gain a little more security, a little more protection,

332
00:21:44.880 --> 00:21:47.119
[S20] a little more control over uncertainty.

333
00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:55.119
[S20] It sounds reasonable until you realize that every major loss of freedom in history began with a reasonable explanation.

334
00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:59.279
[S22] Once somebody says, we need to protect the children, they're in danger.

335
00:22:00.319 --> 00:22:02.559
[S22] It completely bypasses logic.

336
00:22:02.960 --> 00:22:06.400
[S22] It bypasses debate. It bypasses rationality.

337
00:22:06.799 --> 00:22:09.519
[S22] All of a sudden, people are ready to give up everything.

338
00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:17.039
[S20] The justification changes, the language changes, the technology changes, but the trade remains the same.

339
00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:24.640
[S20] Freedom exchanged for security, privacy exchanged for convenience, rights exchanged for promises.

340
00:22:25.269 --> 00:22:28.640
[S20] And today, that trade is happening faster than ever before.

341
00:22:29.099 --> 00:22:32.940
[S23] Porque, por primera vez en la historia de los humanos,

342
00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:36.059
[S23] la vigilancia no necesita miles de personas.

343
00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:37.880
[S23] Necesita algoritmos.

344
00:22:38.539 --> 00:22:39.579
[S23] Necesita servidores.

345
00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:42.619
[S23] Necesita inteligencia artificial.

346
00:22:43.099 --> 00:22:43.819
[S23] Piensa en eso.

347
00:22:44.299 --> 00:22:49.009
[S23] En el pasado, los gobiernos necesitaban enormes recursos para monitorear a millones de personas.

348
00:22:49.579 --> 00:22:52.619
[S23] Hoy, la tecnología puede hacerlo automáticamente.

349
00:22:53.099 --> 00:23:00.130
[S23] Cada búsqueda, cada mensaje, cada interacción, cada relación, cada opinión,

350
00:23:00.619 --> 00:23:02.299
[S23] potencialmente almacenada para siempre.

351
00:23:02.779 --> 00:23:04.130
[S23] Pero aquí está la parte extraña.

352
00:23:04.619 --> 00:23:05.880
[S23] La mayoría de la gente no se preocupa.

353
00:23:06.460 --> 00:23:08.930
[S23] Porque la libertad no desaparece de la forma que imaginamos.

354
00:23:09.420 --> 00:23:11.420
[S23] No desaparece en un momento dramático.

355
00:23:11.890 --> 00:23:12.619
[S23] Se desvanece.

356
00:23:13.259 --> 00:23:19.339
[S23] Una política, una regulación, una excepción, un compromiso a la vez.

357
00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.460
[S23] Y antes de mucho, lo extraordinario se convierte en normal.

358
00:23:23.019 --> 00:23:25.099
[S23] Lo inaceptable se convierte en aceptable.

359
00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:27.339
[S23] Lo temporal se convierte en permanente.

360
00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:30.130
[S23] Así es como crece cada sistema de control.

361
00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:34.460
[S23] No a través de la fuerza solo, sino a través de la indiferencia pública.

362
00:23:34.779 --> 00:23:39.579
[S24] Durante cinco años, la Comisión Europea ha estado tratando de

363
00:23:41.339 --> 00:23:49.500
[S24] forzar un nuevo conjunto de leyes que requerirían que todas las aplicaciones de mensaje implementaran la puerta trasera a su encriptación.

364
00:23:49.890 --> 00:23:54.220
[S24] Así que todo lo que compartimos en línea, cada mensaje privado,

365
00:23:55.740 --> 00:24:02.619
[S24] cada foto privada que enviamos utilizando aplicaciones como Telegram, WhatsApp o cualquier otra,

366
00:24:03.339 --> 00:24:04.940
[S24] sería automáticamente monitoreado.

367
00:24:05.660 --> 00:24:07.420
[S23] Esta no es una historia sobre política.

368
00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:09.420
[S23] No se trata de izquierda versus derecha.

369
00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:11.019
[S23] No se trata de países.

370
00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:14.460
[S23] Se trata de una pregunta que afecta a todas las personas vivas.

371
00:24:14.859 --> 00:24:19.819
[S23] ¿Cuánta privacidad estamos dispuestos a perder antes de que nos demos cuenta de su valor?

372
00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:22.380
[S23] Porque la privacidad no se trata de esconder crímenes.

373
00:24:22.859 --> 00:24:24.700
[S23] La privacidad se trata de ser humano.

374
00:24:25.259 --> 00:24:27.890
[S23] Es la habilidad de pensar libremente.

375
00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:36.059
[S23] Hablar honestamente, explorar ideas sin miedo, discordar, aprender, cambiar tu mente,

376
00:24:36.619 --> 00:24:37.890
[S23] sin sentirse vigilado.

377
00:24:38.460 --> 00:24:41.819
[S23] Y una vez que desaparece, algo más desaparece con él.

378
00:24:42.220 --> 00:24:45.890
[S23] Creatividad, innovación, pensamiento independiente.

379
00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:48.299
[S23] Las bases de una sociedad libre.

380
00:24:48.859 --> 00:24:54.619
[S23] Durante toda la historia, el progreso fue impulsado por la gente dispuesta a desafiar las ideas populares.

381
00:24:55.099 --> 00:24:57.890
[S23] La gente dispuesta a hablar cuando otros permanecen silenciosos.

382
00:24:58.299 --> 00:25:01.579
[S23] La gente dispuesta a cuestionar la autoridad.

383
00:25:02.059 --> 00:25:03.819
[S23] Pero imagina si todos los pensamientos estuvieran monitoreados.

384
00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:06.779
[S23] Cada conversación analizada.

385
00:25:07.339 --> 00:25:09.500
[S23] Cada opinión marcada.

386
00:25:10.059 --> 00:25:12.380
[S23] ¿Esperan que esas ideas existan?

387
00:25:12.859 --> 00:25:15.819
[S23] Por esto es que la libertad digital importa.

388
00:25:16.380 --> 00:25:22.299
[S23] No por la tecnología, sino porque la tecnología ahora forma casi toda parte de la vida humana.

389
00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:27.180
[S23] La batalla por la libertad no se está haciendo solo en las calles y parlamentos.

390
00:25:27.660 --> 00:25:35.339
[S23] Se está haciendo en pantallas, en mensajes, dentro de los espacios digitales donde viven miles de personas todos los días.

391
00:25:36.059 --> 00:25:41.740
[S24] Los criminales serán los primeros en encender otras herramientas más seguras.

392
00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:45.980
[S24] Usarán VPNs, usarán aplicaciones privadas secretas.

393
00:25:46.460 --> 00:25:49.099
[S23] Y es por eso que algunas personas eligen resistir.

394
00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:53.660
[S23] No porque sea fácil, no porque sea rentable,

395
00:25:54.140 --> 00:25:58.619
[S23] sino porque ciertos principios se vuelven sin valor en el momento en que se detiene de defenderlos.

396
00:25:59.019 --> 00:26:03.819
[S23] Durante casi dos décadas, Pavel Durov ha construido plataformas alrededor de una simple creencia:

397
00:26:04.299 --> 00:26:06.940
[S23] Esa comunicación privada debe permanecer privada.

398
00:26:07.500 --> 00:26:09.660
[S23] Esa libertad de expresión importa.

399
00:26:10.220 --> 00:26:13.500
[S23] Esa tecnología debe empoderar a la gente en lugar de controlarlas.

400
00:26:13.579 --> 00:26:18.619
[S24] Las libertades personales han sido erodidas en todo el mundo, casi sin excepción.

401
00:26:18.779 --> 00:26:24.700
[S24] He visto todos los trucos, los trucos de la PR, los trucos legales, los trucos políticos.

402
00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:28.140
[S24] Nuestro barco ya ha llegado al iceberg.

403
00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:31.819
[S24] Ya hemos empezado a caer.

404
00:26:31.890 --> 00:26:34.700
[S24] Y estoy hablando de la nave de nuestras libertades personales.

405
00:26:35.579 --> 00:26:37.819
[S23] Tal vez esté equivocado.

406
00:26:38.299 --> 00:26:39.500
[S23] La historia decidirá eso.

407
00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:48.140
[S23] Pero una cosa es segura: cada generación finalmente enfrenta un momento en el que tiene que decidir qué tipo de futuro quiere dejar atrás.

408
00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:50.140
[S23] Un futuro construido sobre la libertad.

409
00:26:50.700 --> 00:26:53.660
[S23] O un futuro construido sobre el control.

410
00:26:54.049 --> 00:26:57.019
[S23] Y la elección raramente obvia mientras se está sucediendo.

411
00:26:57.500 --> 00:26:59.019
[S23] Por eso es que el Titanic importa.

412
00:26:59.579 --> 00:27:03.500
[S23] Por eso el iceberg, y por eso esperar es tan peligroso.

413
00:27:04.049 --> 00:27:06.859
[S23] Porque los pasajeros del Titanic todavía tenían barcos de vidas.

